In ASP.NET Core dependency injection (DI), AddSingleton, AddScoped, and AddTransient are methods used to register services with the DI container. The main differences are:

1. AddSingleton registers a service as a singleton, meaning a single instance is created for the lifetime of the application. This instance is shared across all requests and lifetime scope.

2. AddScoped registers a service as scoped, meaning a new instance is created for each request lifetime. This instance is shared within a single request but not across different requests.

3. AddTransient registers a service as transient, meaning a new instance is created every time a dependency is requested. Each request gets its own instance of the service.

Here's an example:

```csharp
// AddSingleton
services.AddSingleton<ISomeService, SomeService>();

// AddScoped
services.AddScoped<ISomeService, SomeService>();

// AddTransient
services.AddTransient<ISomeService, SomeService>();
```

To register a repository per request in ASP.NET Core, you can use the Scoped lifetime for the repository interface and the transient lifetime for the concrete implementation. Here's an example:

```csharp
// Startup.cs
public void ConfigureServices(IServiceCollection services)
{
    services.AddScoped<IRepository, Repository>();
    services.AddTransient<SomeController>();
}

// SomeController.cs
public class SomeController : Controller
{
    private readonly IRepository _repository;

    public SomeController(IRepository repository)
    {
        _repository = repository;
    }
}
```

In this example, the `IRepository` interface is registered with the Scoped lifetime, meaning a new instance of the repository will be created for each request. The `SomeController` constructor injects an instance of the repository, which will be a new instance for each request.

To return a 404 status code from a minimal API endpoint in ASP.NET Core, you can use the `HttpContext.Response.StatusCode` property and then call `HttpContext.Response.Body.WriteAsync` with the appropriate response bytes. Here's an example:

```csharp
app.MapGet("/nonexistent", (HttpContext httpContext) =>
{
    httpContext.Response.StatusCode = 404;
    await httpContext.Response.Body.WriteAsync(Encoding.UTF8.GetBytes("Not Found"));
});
```

In this code, we define a minimal API endpoint using `app.MapGet` that responds with a 404 status code and a "Not Found" message when accessed. The `httpContext.Response.StatusCode` sets the HTTP status code to 404, indicating that the requested resource was not found. The `await httpContext.Response.Body.WriteAsync` method writes the "Not Found" message to the response body.

The recommended way to create HttpClient instances in ASP.NET Core is to use dependency injection (DI) to register and resolve the HttpClient instances throughout your application. This ensures that a single instance of HttpClient is reused across requests, improving performance and allowing for proper configuration and management of the HttpClient.

Here's an example of how you can register and use an HttpClient in ASP.NET Core:

```csharp
public void ConfigureServices(IServiceCollection services)
{
    services.AddHttpClient<ITwitterClient, TwitterClient>();
    // ...
}

public class Startup
{
    public void Configure(IApplicationBuilder app, IWebHostEnvironment env)
    {
        // ...
        app.UseEndpoints(endpoints =>
        {
            endpoints.MapControllers();
        });
    }
}
```

In this example, the `AddHttpClient<ITwitterClient, TwitterClient>()` method registers an HttpClient named `ITwitterClient` with its corresponding implementation `TwitterClient`. The `AddHttpClient` method automatically configures the HttpClient with appropriate defaults and allows for customization through the `ConfigurePrimaryHttpMessageHandler` method if needed.

To load related entities eagerly in EF Core, you can use the Include method in your LINQ query. For example, if you have a Blog entity with related Posts, you can load the posts eagerly like this:

