This will load the posts along with the blogs in a single query.

In ASP.NET Core, the options interface that recomputes settings for each request is the `IOptionsSnapshot<T>` interface. It allows you to retrieve options that are recomputed for each request, ensuring that the latest configuration values are used. Here's an example:

```csharp
public class MyOptions
{
    public string Setting1 { get; set; }
    public int Setting2 { get; set; }
}

public class MyController : Controller
{
    private readonly IOptionsSnapshot<MyOptions> _optionsSnapshot;

    public MyController(IOptionsSnapshot<MyOptions> optionsSnapshot)
    {
        _optionsSnapshot = optionsSnapshot;
    }

    public IActionResult Index()
    {
        var settings = _optionsSnapshot.Value;
        // Use the recomputed settings for each request
        // ...
    }
}
```

In this example, `IOptionsSnapshot<MyOptions>` is used to inject the options into the controller constructor. The `_optionsSnapshot.Value` property retrieves the current settings for each request, ensuring that the latest values are used.

In ASP.NET Core, you can use the IHostedService interface to implement long-running background work. Here's an example:

```csharp
public class MyBackgroundService : IHostedService
{
    private readonly ILogger<MyBackgroundService> _logger;

    public MyBackgroundService(ILogger<MyBackgroundService> logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;
    }

    public Task StartAsync(CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        _logger.LogInformation("Background service started.");

        // Perform long-running background work here
        // ...

        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    public Task StopAsync(CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        _logger.LogInformation("Background service stopped.");

        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

In this example, the `MyBackgroundService` class implements the `IHostedService` interface. The `StartAsync` method is called when the service is started, and the `StopAsync` method is called when the service is stopped. You can perform your long-running background work within these methods.

To allow a caller to cancel an async method in C#, you can use the `CancellationToken` parameter. Here's an example:

```csharp
public async Task MyAsyncMethod(CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
    while (!cancellationToken.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        // Perform some work
        await Task.Delay(1000, cancellationToken);
    }
}
```

In this example, the `MyAsyncMethod` takes a `CancellationToken` parameter. Inside the method, you can periodically check the `IsCancellationRequested` property of the token to determine if the caller has requested cancellation. If cancellation is requested, the method will exit the loop and return.

The caller can then call the async method with a cancellation token, and if needed, they can trigger cancellation using the `CancellationTokenSource`:

```csharp
var cancellationTokenSource = new CancellationTokenSource();
var cancellationToken = cancellationTokenSource.Token;

// Start the async method
await MyAsyncMethod(cancellationToken);

// Trigger cancellation if needed
cancellationTokenSource.Cancel();
```

By using the `CancellationToken` and `CancellationTokenSource`, you can provide a way for the caller to cancel the async method gracefully.

